    "wall_seconds": 2.791489144001389,
    "content": "\n\nTo a compiler engineer, speculative decoding is best understood not as a change to the model's logic, but as a **speculative execution** strategy applied to the inference loop, analogous to out-of-order execution or branch prediction in a CPU.\n\nThe core problem it solves is that Large Language Model (LLM) inference is **memory-bandwidth bound**, not compute-bound. Generating a single token requires loading the entire model's weights (potentially tens of gigabytes) from HBM (High Bandwidth Memory) into the GPU's SRAM/registers. Whether you generate one token or a batch of 100 tokens, the cost of moving that data is roughly the same. Therefore, the GPU's massive compute units (Tensor Cores) sit idle while waiting for memory.\n\nSpeculative decoding exploits this by decoupling the \"generation\" of tokens from the \"verification\" of tokens.\n\n### The Architecture: Draft and Verify\n\nThe system uses two models:\n1.  **The Draft Model (Speculator):** A small, fast model (e.g., a 7B parameter model) that generates $k$ candidate tokens sequentially. Because it is small, it is compute-bound or at least much faster per token than the large model.",
