    "wall_seconds": 2.008562321017962,
    "content": "\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n    customer_id,\n    order_date,\n    order_amount,\n    SUM(order_amount) OVER (\n        PARTITION BY customer_id\n        ORDER BY order_date\n        ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW\n    ) AS rolling_7_day_revenue\nFROM orders;\n```\n\n**Window frame explanation:**\n\n- **PARTITION BY customer_id** \u2014 each customer's rows are treated as a separate window.\n- **ORDER BY order_date** \u2014 rows within each partition are ordered chronologically.\n- **ROWS BETWEEN 6 PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW** \u2014 the frame includes the current row plus the 6 rows immediately before it (7 rows total).\n\n**Important caveat:** This uses `ROWS`, not `RANGE`. `ROWS` counts *physical rows*, not calendar days. If a customer has multiple orders on the same day or gaps in their order history, the frame will not correspond to a true 7-calendar-day window.\n\nFor a true rolling 7-calendar-day total, use `RANGE` with an interval (supported in PostgreSQL, SQL Server 2012+, etc.):\n\n```sql\nSUM(order_amount) OVER (\n    PARTITION BY",
