    "wall_seconds": 3.3174840890278574,
    "content": "\n\nBelow is a compact but complete treatment: the failure modes you are likely hitting, and a shutdown protocol that is safe under the usual memory model (C++/Java/Go semantics). I\u2019ll assume a classic MPMC queue with a head/tail index, a fixed ring buffer, and a \"closed\" flag.\n\nLikely races that cause lost work\n\n1) Close-then-drain race (most common)\n- Producer: if (!closed) { push(item); }\n- Consumer: if (closed) break; else pop();\n- Race: A producer checks closed == false, then is preempted. Another thread closes the queue. The consumer sees closed and exits, draining nothing. The producer resumes and pushes. That item is never consumed.\n- Root cause: \"closed\" is not part of the atomicity of the push/pop operation; visibility of closed is not ordered with the data.\n\n2) Double-close / inconsistent state\n- Multiple threads call close(); one sets closed=true, another also manipulates state (e.g., resets tail, frees buffer). Without synchronization, you can corrupt indices or free memory while a producer is mid-push.\n\n3) Memory ordering issues\n- Using relaxed atomics for closed and/or head",
